<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
	PHP coding-standard ruleset for the Blitzy MediaWiki skin.

	The "phpcs" Composer script runs PHP_CodeSniffer with no path argument, so the
	<file> scope declared below is exactly what gets checked. PHP_CodeSniffer is one
	of the five linters the zero-warning build gate requires to exit cleanly, which
	means a single reported warning fails the build, not just a reported error.

	The MediaWiki standard is referenced whole, and no sniff property is overridden.
	When a sniff fires, the PHP is corrected rather than the sniff silenced. That
	keeps the docblock, spacing, naming and test-annotation sniffs in force on every
	PHP file this skin owns, and it keeps this ruleset free of the grandfathering
	exclusions an older tree accumulates.

	There is exactly one exclusion, declared at the foot of this file. It is scoped
	to a single sniff MESSAGE CODE on a single named file, it is not a directory or
	sniff-wide silencing, and it exists because the conflict it resolves cannot be
	resolved in the PHP at all. Read the comment there before adding a second one:
	the bar is that no correction to the code could satisfy the sniff.

	Static analysis is configured separately by the "phan" Composer script through
	its own configuration flag, so no static-analysis settings belong in this file.
-->
<ruleset name="Blitzy">
	<description>The MediaWiki coding standard, applied to every PHP file in the Blitzy skin.</description>

	<!--
		The standard itself, resolved relative to this file's directory. Its exact
		version is pinned in composer.json and deliberately not repeated here, so
		the pin has exactly one home and upgrading stays a one-manifest change.
	-->
	<rule ref="./vendor/mediawiki/mediawiki-codesniffer/MediaWiki" />

	<!--
		Scope is this package and nothing above it. The skin never reaches outside
		its own directory, so a run can only ever report on files the skin owns.
	-->
	<file>.</file>

	<!--
		Check PHP only. PHP_CodeSniffer otherwise defaults to php, inc, js and css,
		which would pull the skin's JavaScript into this run even though ESLint
		already owns it, and would apply a deprecated tokenizer while doing so. The
		Mustache templates, Less stylesheets and JSON manifests are likewise checked
		by their own tools, so this run stays PHP and nothing else.
	-->
	<arg name="extensions" value="php"/>
	<arg name="encoding" value="UTF-8"/>

	<!--
		Installed dependency trees contain no authored code. These two patterns
		mirror the exclusions the "test" Composer script hands to the PHP syntax
		linter, so both PHP checkers walk an identical file set and nothing ends up
		checked by one tool but skipped by the other. They are also the two trees
		.gitignore keeps out of the repository.

		type="relative" anchors each pattern at the package root. An unanchored
		pattern is matched against the whole absolute path instead, and would
		silently suppress every file in the package if any parent directory happened
		to be named vendor.
	-->
	<exclude-pattern type="relative">^vendor/*</exclude-pattern>
	<exclude-pattern type="relative">^node_modules/*</exclude-pattern>

	<!--
		The single exclusion, scoped to one sniff message code on one file.

		MediaWiki.Files.ClassMatchesFilename.NotMatch requires a file's class name to
		equal its filename. harness/post-import-verify.php is a maintenance script,
		and the technical specification fixes both its path and its kebab-case name:
		harness/LocalSettings.template.php refers to it by that name, harness/verify.sh
		and harness/provision.sh invoke it by that path, and DELIVERY.md reports its
		results under it. A PHP class name cannot contain a hyphen, so no class name
		exists that would satisfy the sniff — the usual remedy of correcting the code
		is unavailable here, which is the bar this exclusion had to clear.

		The sniff's own escape hatch does not apply either. It forgives a maintenance
		script whose filename differs from its class name only in the case of the first
		letter, class FooBar in fooBar.php, and nothing else; a difference of spelling
		is reported.

		MediaWiki core resolves the identical conflict identically, and at scale. Its
		own .phpcs.xml excludes this same message code for twenty of its own maintenance
		scripts, one of them maintenance/edit.php, whose class is EditCLI, and excludes
		the sibling WrongCase code for a further set, among them maintenance/
		rebuildall.php, whose class is RebuildAll. Excluding the code in the ruleset is
		also what keeps the PHP free of an inline phpcs:ignore directive, which the
		zero-warning build gate prohibits.

		Scope is deliberately as narrow as PHP_CodeSniffer allows: the message code
		rather than the sniff, and one named path rather than the harness directory.
		Every other file in the package, including every other PHP file under harness/,
		is still required to name its class after itself — verified by checking that a
		second mis-named file in the same directory still fails.

		The pattern is written in the leading-wildcard form core uses rather than with
		type="relative" like the two above, and that is not cosmetic: a relative pattern
		is matched against the path relative to the base path, which the "phpcs"
		Composer script does not pass on the command line, so a relative pattern here
		would silently never match and the exclusion would not apply at all. The two
		patterns above are unaffected because they exclude trees that hold no authored
		code either way.
	-->
	<rule ref="MediaWiki.Files.ClassMatchesFilename.NotMatch">
		<exclude-pattern>*/harness/post-import-verify\.php</exclude-pattern>
	</rule>
</ruleset>
